{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "ואתה תצוה", :translit "v'atah tetzaveh", :gloss "and you shall command"} {:he "מחני נא מספרך אשר כתבת", :translit "m'cheni na mispir'cha asher katavta", :gloss "blot me out I pray from Your book which You have written"} {:he "צוואה", :translit "tzavah", :gloss "command/testament"} {:he "וקללת חכם אפי' על תנאי באה", :translit "uk'lalat chakham afi al ta'nai ba'ah", :gloss "and the curse of a sage even on a condition comes"} {:he "צו את בני ישראל", :translit "tzav et b'nei yisra'el", :gloss "command the children of Israel"} {:he "תצוה", :translit "tetzaveh", :gloss "you shall command"} {:he "נשים צוה", :translit "nashim tzivah", :gloss "command to women"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :gematria, :a "תצוה", :b "נשים צוה"}], :frame {:text "Moses said to God, 'Blot me out, I pray, from Your book which You have written' (Exodus 32:32). The Talmud states that the curse of a sage takes effect even if it was conditional: Makkot 11a records that Rabbi Yishmael ben Elisha said that the decree of a sage, even if conditional, comes to fulfillment. Because Moses made this statement after the sin of the Golden Calf (Exodus 32), his name is absent from this entire sidra as its fulfillment. The kindling of Shabbat lamps is a rabbinic obligation specifically incumbent upon women, as stated in Shabbat 31b and codified in Mishneh Shabbat 2:6.", :sources ["Exodus 32:32" "Makkot 11a" "Shabbat 31b" "Mishneh Shabbat 2:6"]}} {:quotes [{:he "ויקחו אליך", :translit "v'yikchu eilecha", :gloss "and they shall take to you"} {:he "אבל אני איני צריך לאורה", :translit "aval ani eino tzarich l'orah", :gloss "but I do not need its light"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Midrashic reading: 'to you' implies God does not need the Menorah's light for Himself"}], :frame {:text "God does not require the Menorah's light, as stated in Numbers Rabbah 15:7 and similarly in Tanchuma Beha'alot'cha 5. The purpose of the Menorah is symbolic — to provide light for Israel, not for God, who is Himself the source of light. The phrase 'to you' underscores that the light serves people, not the divine.", :sources ["Numbers Rabbah 15:7" "Tanchuma Beha'alot'cha 5"]}} {:quotes [{:he "כתית", :translit "katit", :gloss "beaten/crushed"} {:he "ת\"י ת\"כ", :translit "tav yud tav kaf", :gloss "Tav-Yud Tav-Kaf abbreviation"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Notarikon-like hint: ת\"י ת\"כ means 'two,' hinting at two daily kindlings of the Menorah"}], :frame {:text "The Menorah was kindled twice daily, corresponding to the morning and evening service, as described in Exodus 30:7-8 where Aaron tends the lamps morning and evening. The daily kindling is further elaborated in Tamid 3:9 and the Talmud in Shabbat 24b which discusses the relationship between the Menorah's lighting and the daily tamid offering.", :sources ["Exodus 30:7-8" "Tamid 3:9" "Shabbat 24b"]}} {:quotes [{:he "נר תמיד", :translit "ner tamid", :gloss "a perpetual lamp"} {:he "בשבת", :translit "b'shabbat", :gloss "on Shabbat"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :gematria, :a "נר תמיד", :b "בשבת"}], :frame {:text "The perpetual lamp (ner tamid) of the Menorah is commanded in Exodus 27:20-21. The practice of having a perpetual light in the synagogue derives from this command, as discussed in Shabbat 22b. The obligation to kindle lamps specifically for Shabbat is a distinct rabbinic command discussed in Shabbat 23b-25b.", :sources ["Exodus 27:20-21" "Shabbat 22b" "Shabbat 23b"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :gematria, :a "תצוה", :a-value 501, :b "נשים צוה", :b-value 501, :lens :standard, :by :selah, :equal? true} {:kind :other, :note "Midrashic reading: 'to you' implies God does not need the Menorah's light for Himself", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "Notarikon-like hint: ת\"י ת\"כ means 'two,' hinting at two daily kindlings of the Menorah", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :gematria, :a "נר תמיד", :a-value 704, :b "בשבת", :b-value 704, :lens :standard, :by :selah, :equal? true}], :book :exodus, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:19:49.211125095Z", :model "glm-5.2"}